					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "COLOR OFFSET\n\nInstead of using floats for each channel we use switches and booleans to let the user decide which channels to offset. This way it is easy to shift two channels at the same time using just one slider, making the effect more subtle in the process.\n\nNote that we use a pack to convert the Float into a Float 2. This is because we only want to shift on the X-axis for this effect. But this is something you can easily change (yes, I challenge you!)\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 725.24493408203125,
					"width": 1386.0352783203125,
					"x": -685.289306640625,
					"y": -441.3668212890625
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffff6a00",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"85": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "GENERATING A TEXTURE\n\nWire can convert values like integers, floats and booleans to textures using the Texture node.\nEach value will be translated to a single pixel.\n\nWe start out with a Gradient Palette node to generate  colors(float 4 values).\nWhen you select the colors in the Gradient Palette node you should note that the blue and red channels are at 0. \nWe want only green colors for the UV offset node later on. \nWhy will be explained when we get to that node.\n\nWe Shuffle the collection of Float 4 values.\nIf we don’t do this we end up with a gradient instead of random colors.\n\nAfter this we convert the collection of float 4 values to a texture, using the Texture node.\n\nClick on the Texture node and have a look at the bottom of your monitor.\nYou will notice that the textures are abywhere between 5 x 1 and 20 x 1 pixels in size.\nThese textures are really small, which is great as they won’t eat up a lot of VRAM.\nFinally we Resize the miniature textures to fill the entire screen. \nNote that the sampling method of the Resize node is set to nearest. \nFeel free to change it around and see what the results look like."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 775.3621826171875,
					"width": 1386.1798095703125,
					"x": -685.433837890625,
					"y": 296.7188720703125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "fff26eb5",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"86": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "UV OFFSET\n\nIn the final stage of the patch we take the color shifted texture and send it to the UV Offset to be… well, offsetted. The texture to offset it with is the texture we generated from the Gradient Palette below.\n\nThe UV Offset node takes the offset texture and looks at the red and green values of each pixel.\nNext it offsets the incoming texture horizontally depending on the amount of red in the offset texture. The same is true vertically, depending on the green in the offset texture.\nBy now you should be able to guess why we made sure our Gradient Palette only contained colors that have no values in red and green channels.\n\nWe used a Float in and a multiply node to get a value running from 0 to 0.1 to act as an amplifier for the amount of movement in the effect. You should definitely take some time to play with the settings of the UV Offset. By now you should be able to create some textures yourself to send to the UV Offset node, can you make this patch work horizontally?\n"
					}
				},
